# Copy to `.env` and edit. `.env` is gitignored - never commit real values.
#   cp .env.example .env

# --- Optional for the default tier (OpenEMR only) ----------------------------

# OpenEMR admin password, set at first boot. Defaults to `LocalDev1!` if unset
# (see docker-compose.yml) so `docker compose up` works with zero config. This is
# a throwaway local default, deliberately not a real credential. Set it here
# only if you want a different one, or before exposing the stack beyond localhost.
# OPENEMR_ADMIN_PASSWORD=LocalDev1!

# Host port for the front door. Everything is reached through this one origin - and since
# a separate change that includes the sidecar, which runs inside the proxy's network namespace and
# calls OpenEMR back through `http://localhost:${DEMO_PORT}` like the browser does. So nginx
# now LISTENS on this port inside the container as well as being published on it; moving it
# moves both halves together, and there is nothing left that only the browser sees.
#
# IT CANNOT BE SIDECAR_PORT (8081 below). That used to be a host-side number only, and any value
# was safe; now it is nginx's listen port in the SAME namespace as the sidecar's Kestrel, and 8081
# is the obvious next number to reach for when 8080 is taken. Both would want the one port: nginx
# starts first and wins it, Kestrel crash-loops on "address already in use", and / keeps serving
# OpenEMR while /agentforge/* 502s - which is what a forgotten `--profile copilot` also looks like.
# The proxy refuses to start rather than serve that, naming both knobs (reverse-proxy's
# 15-assert-sidecar-port.sh), so the mistake is loud - but pick a different number and skip it.
DEMO_PORT=8080

# The sidecar's own Kestrel port INSIDE that shared namespace, and it cannot be DEMO_PORT (above).
# Everything that has to agree with it is derived from it, so moving it moves them together:
# Kestrel's PORT, the proxy's SIDECAR_UPSTREAM, and - under
# docker-compose.observability.yml - Prometheus's SIDECAR_TARGET, which is why that overlay builds
# observability/prometheus/Dockerfile rather than bind-mounting a static config into stock
# prom/prometheus. A literal port there would keep scraping 8081 while everything else moved, and the
# only symptom would be blank dashboards behind a `/ready` that still says Healthy.
# Change it only if 8081 collides with the front-door port you chose.
# SIDECAR_PORT=8081

# --- Required for `--profile copilot` (the sidecar) --------------------------

# The sidecar's Llm:ApiKey is [Required] and validated at startup, so it CANNOT
# boot without this. Get one at https://console.anthropic.com/.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# A confidential SMART client registered in OpenEMR *and enabled by an admin* -
# freshly-registered clients land disabled. See the README bootstrap steps.
OPENEMR_CLIENT_ID=
OPENEMR_CLIENT_SECRET=

# --- Optional ----------------------------------------------------------------

# Override any of the three first-party images. TWO OF THE THREE NOW DEFAULT TO AN EXPLICIT
# PIN: OPENEMR_IMAGE to an `openemr-sha-<12>`, and SIDECAR_IMAGE to the very
# `agent-forge-sha-<12>` production runs - docker-compose.yml MIRRORS THE DEPLOYMENT rather
# than tracking the newest build; keep the two in step. Wanting the newest build instead is development, and it has its own file:
# docker-compose.dev.yml, which follows `agent-forge-latest`.
#
# PROXY_IMAGE IS THE ONE STILL ON A MOVING `-latest` TAG - and that is a choice
# rather than a shortage. `publish-proxy-image` (GitLab, every `develop` push) has run, and
# production pins an explicit `proxy-sha-<12>` (.railway/railway.ts, PROXY_IMAGE_BY_ENV). This default still says `proxy-latest`,
# a hand-pushed workstation build the job deliberately does not move (DEPLOYMENT.md section 7);
# aligning it with the deployment is a separate change. Compose BUILDS the proxy from ./reverse-proxy unless you pull, so
# that default is rarely what runs; set it to an explicit `-sha-<12>` when it has to be.
#
# `openemr-latest` IS NOT WHAT THE PIN BELOW USES - do not substitute it. See
# DEPLOYMENT.md section 1 "Publishing status" for why. `openemr-main`
# does not exist at all - asking for it fails with `manifest not found`.
# OPENEMR_IMAGE=docker.io/amarquette/gauntletai:openemr-sha-<12>
# SIDECAR_IMAGE=docker.io/amarquette/gauntletai:agent-forge-sha-<12>
# PROXY_IMAGE=docker.io/amarquette/gauntletai:proxy-sha-<12>

# A fourth, used only by docker-compose.observability.yml, and NOT a `-latest` default: that overlay
# builds observability/prometheus/Dockerfile under the local tag `agent-forge-prometheus:local`, so
# nothing pulls the unprovenanced `prometheus-latest` by accident (DEPLOYMENT.md section 7). Set this
# to run a published build instead. Either way the scrape target is rendered from SIDECAR_PORT.
# PROMETHEUS_IMAGE=docker.io/amarquette/gauntletai:prometheus-sha-<12>

# The prices are claude-sonnet-5's list rate and are only as good as the model they are
# paired with - nothing validates the pair, so change all three together.
# LLM_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5
# LLM_INPUT_PRICE=2.00
# LLM_OUTPUT_PRICE=10.00

# Local-only database passwords. Fine to leave at the compose defaults for a
# throwaway demo; set them if the ports are exposed anywhere shared.
# MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=rootpass
# MYSQL_PASSWORD=openemr
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=agentforge

# Grafana's admin login. BOTH observability wirings read the same two variable NAMES -
# docker-compose.observability.yml (the overlay that runs Prometheus/Loki/Grafana inside the
# main stack) and observability/docker-compose.yml (the separate stack, for a host-run
# sidecar) - but they do NOT read the same file; see below.
# Under the overlay Grafana sits on the same network as the databases, so this login is
# what stands between :3000 and them - see DEPLOYMENT.md. Both
# publish on 127.0.0.1 only, so the defaults are safe for a local run; CHANGE THESE
# before publishing the port anywhere else.
# SET HERE, THIS REACHES THE OVERLAY ONLY. Compose resolves the default .env from the first
# compose file's directory, so the overlay (first file: ./docker-compose.yml) reads this file
# with no flag, while `-f observability/docker-compose.yml` resolves its .env from
# observability/ and never reads this one - Grafana stays on admin/admin without saying so.
# The separate stack's credential belongs in observability/.env (see observability/.env.example);
# to drive it from this file instead, add `--env-file .env` to every bring-up, and note that the
# flag makes the file MANDATORY where the default .env is optional.
# GRAFANA_ADMIN_USER=admin
# GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=admin

# --- Readiness: the observability backend ------------------------------------
#
# `Observability__PrometheusHealthUrl` IS NOT A `.env` VARIABLE, and putting it here would
# have no effect: nothing in docker-compose.yml interpolates it, exactly as with
# `Observability__LokiOtlpEndpoint`. It is listed here because it is the one input to
# `/ready` an operator can be surprised by. It is set for you by
# docker-compose.observability.yml (to `http://prometheus:9090/-/healthy`) and, where an
# environment actually deploys Prometheus, by .railway/railway.ts. A sidecar run on the host
# with `dotnet run` gets it from nothing by default - export it yourself, e.g.
# `http://localhost:9090/-/healthy` against observability/docker-compose.yml.
#
# THE CONSEQUENCE OF LEAVING IT UNSET, which is the default and is fine: `/ready` reports
# **Degraded**, not Healthy, because the third dependency NFR-HEALTH-1 names was never
# contacted. ASP.NET Core maps Degraded to **HTTP 200**, so an uptime check that reads only
# the status code cannot tell it apart from a full pass - read the body, which is JSON naming
# every check and its status, so it says which one degraded. That is deliberate
# (ARCHITECTURE.md D17, at the repo root): the observability stack is optional infra, and
# failing readiness over it would pull a working sidecar out of rotation. OpenEMR or the LLM
# provider being unreachable is a real 503. The vector index is the other check that can read
# Degraded, and for the same reason - see AgentForgeData__ConnectionString in DEPLOYMENT.md.
#
# SETTING IT WRONG IS NOT THE SAME CASE. The carve-out above is for "never configured" only:
# once the URL is set, a Prometheus that is unreachable or silent makes `/ready` a real 503
# too - promptly, within `Readiness__ProbeTimeout`, rather than after HttpClient's
# 100-second default. Point it at one you expect to answer, or leave it unset.

# --- Traces: the OTLP trace exporter ---------------------------------
#
# `Observability__TraceOtlpEndpoint` IS NOT A `.env` VARIABLE either - nothing in docker-compose.yml
# interpolates it. docker-compose.observability.yml sets it to `http://tempo:4318/v1/traces`, beside
# the Tempo it adds; .railway/railway.ts sets it only where it declares a `tempo` service (staging,
# once its pin is filled - never production, the disposition). The base stack runs no Tempo and
# sets nothing, so the sidecar registers NO trace exporter at all: unset is the transparent default,
# not a fault, and /ready never probes Tempo. `Observability__TraceConsoleExporter=true` prints spans
# to stdout for local debugging; no deployed environment sets it. Every exported span is
# scrubbed of identifiers first (SpanPhiScrubber; ARCHITECTURE-DOCUMENTS.md section 12).

# --- Security platform — not read by docker-compose -----------------------
#
# The deterministic replayer image is not a compose service. These are the knobs
# `python -m security_platform run` reads; no CI job runs it (live runs are a separate change). The committed
# allowlist is the staging front door, env may only narrow it, and the production origin is refused
# in code. No model API key. DEPLOYMENT.md has the command.
# SECURITY_PLATFORM_TARGET_URL=https://reverse-proxy-staging-5c25.up.railway.app/
# SECURITY_PLATFORM_ALLOWLIST=https://reverse-proxy-staging-5c25.up.railway.app/
# SECURITY_PLATFORM_SPEND_CEILING_USD=1
# SECURITY_PLATFORM_USD_PER_REQUEST=0.001
